orticoid receptors are indispensable for nongenomic modulation of hippocampal glutamate transmission by corticosterone.</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><volume>102</volume><fpage>19204</fpage><lpage>19207</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16361444</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004714-Wiegert1"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wiegert</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Pu</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Shor</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Jo&#x000eb;ls</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Krugers</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Glucocorticoid receptor activation selectively hampers N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor dependent hippocampal synaptic plasticity in vitro.</article-title><source>Neuroscience</source><volume>135</volume><fpage>403</fpage><lpage>11</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16125856</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004714-Groc1"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Groc</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Choquet</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Chaouloff</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>The stress hormone corticosterone conditions AMPAR surface trafficking and synaptic potentiation.</article-title><source>Nature Neurosci</source><volume>11</volume><fpage>868</fpage><lpage>870</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18622402</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0004714-Hoogenraad1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hoogenraad</surname><given-names>CC</given-names></name><name><surname>Milstein</surname><given-names>AD</given-names></name><name><s